---

<StatePage
  title="Small Estate Affidavit Maricopa County Arizona: 2026 Guide"
  description="How to file a small estate affidavit in Maricopa County, AZ. HB2116 March 2026 thresholds, Maricopa County Recorder address, fees, and required forms."
  canonicalUrl="https://smallestateform.com/small-estate-affidavit/arizona/maricopa/"
  jsonLd={[articleSchema, faqSchema, howToSchema, breadcrumbSchema]}
>

  <!-- Parent breadcrumb link -->
  <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6 mt-8 mb-4">
    <a href="/small-estate-affidavit/arizona/" class="text-[#8B6914] hover:underline text-sm font-medium">← View all Arizona small estate rules</a>
  </div>

  <!-- HB2116 update callout -->
  <div class="bg-[#FFF8E8] border-l-4 border-[#8B6914] p-4 mb-4 max-w-4xl mx-auto">
    <strong>⚡ Updated March 2026 — HB2116</strong> raised Arizona's small estate thresholds. Personal property: $200,000. Real property: $300,000. Most guides still show old limits.
  </div>

  <!-- Featured snippet callout -->
  <div class="bg-[#EEE9E1] border-l-4 border-[#8B6914] p-6 mb-4 max-w-4xl mx-auto">
    <p class="font-medium text-[#2D5016] leading-relaxed">
      In Maricopa County, Arizona, a Small Estate Affidavit for real property must be recorded with the Maricopa County Recorder at 111 S 3rd Ave, Phoenix, AZ 85003. As of March 2026 under HB2116, the thresholds are $200,000 for personal property and $300,000 for real property. A 30-day waiting period applies.
    </p>
  </div>

  <!-- E-E-A-T disclaimer -->
  <div class="bg-[#F7F4EF] border border-[#D4CCC0] rounded-lg p-4 mb-8 max-w-4xl mx-auto text-sm text-[#6B6560]">
    <strong class="text-[#2C2C2A]">Last updated July 2026.</strong> This information reflects ARS §14-3971 as amended by HB2116, effective March 2026. Laws may change. Consult a licensed Arizona attorney for advice specific to your situation.
  </div>

  <!-- H1 -->
  <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6 mb-8">
    <h1 class="italic font-serif text-4xl text-[#2D5016] leading-tight">
      Small Estate Affidavit in Maricopa County, Arizona: Where to File and What to Expect
    </h1>
  </div>

  <!-- Form app -->
  <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6">
    <AffidavitFormApp
      client:load
      documentType="small-estate-affidavit"
      state="Arizona"
      county="Maricopa"
      eligibilityThreshold={200000}
      waitingDays={30}
      statutoryReference="ARS §14-3971 (HB2116, March 2026)"
    />
  </div>

  <!-- Body content -->
  <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6 py-12">

    <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl text-[#2D5016] mb-4">Maricopa County vs. the Rest of Arizona: What's Different</h2>
    <p class="text-[#2C2C2A] leading-relaxed mb-4">
      Arizona's statewide Small Estate Affidavit law (ARS §14-3971) applies in Maricopa County — but the <strong>forms and recording procedures are county-specific</strong>. What works in Pima or Yavapai County may not be the right form for Maricopa.
    </p>
    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-4 mb-8">
      <div class="border border-[#D4CCC0] rounded-xl p-4 bg-[#F7F4EF]">
        <h3 class="font-medium text-[#2D5016] mb-2">Personal property (bank accounts, vehicles)</h3>
        <p class="text-[#2C2C2A] text-sm">The statewide process applies — present the notarized affidavit to the asset holder. No county-specific forms required.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="border border-[#8B6914] rounded-xl p-4 bg-[#FFF8E8]">
        <h3 class="font-medium text-[#2D5016] mb-2">Real property in Maricopa County</h3>
        <p class="text-[#2C2C2A] text-sm">Must record with the <strong>Maricopa County Recorder</strong>, using forms consistent with Maricopa Superior Court probate division requirements.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl text-[#2D5016] mb-4">HB2116: Current Maricopa County Thresholds (March 2026)</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto mb-8">
      <table class="w-full border-collapse text-sm">
        <thead>
          <tr class="bg-[#2D5016] text-[#F7F4EF]">
            <th class="p-3 text-left">Asset Type</th>
            <th class="p-3 text-left">Current Limit</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr class="border-b border-[#D4CCC0]">
            <td class="p-3 font-medium text-[#2D5016]">Personal property</td>
            <td class="p-3 text-[#8B6914] font-medium">$200,000</td>
          </tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-[#D4CCC0] bg-[#F7F4EF]">
            <td class="p-3 font-medium text-[#2D5016]">Real property</td>
            <td class="p-3 text-[#8B6914] font-medium">$300,000</td>
          </tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-[#D4CCC0]">
            <td class="p-3 font-medium text-[#2D5016]">Waiting period</td>
            <td class="p-3 text-[#2C2C2A]">30 days from date of death</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <!-- Maricopa County Recorder info box -->
    <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl text-[#2D5016] mb-4">Where to Record in Maricopa County</h2>
    <div class="bg-[#EEE9E1] border border-[#D4CCC0] rounded-xl p-6 mb-8">
      <h3 class="font-serif text-xl text-[#2D5016] mb-4">Maricopa County Recorder</h3>
      <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-6">
        <div class="space-y-2 text-sm text-[#2C2C2A]">
          <div><strong class="text-[#2D5016]">Address:</strong> 111 S 3rd Ave, Phoenix, AZ 85003</div>
          <div><strong class="text-[#2D5016]">Phone:</strong> (602) 506-3535</div>
          <div><strong class="text-[#2D5016]">Website:</strong> recorder.maricopa.gov</div>
          <div><strong class="text-[#2D5016]">Hours:</strong> Monday–Friday, 8:00 AM – 5:00 PM (confirm before visiting)</div>
        </div>
        <div class="space-y-2 text-sm text-[#2C2C2A]">
          <div class="font-medium text-[#2D5016]">Recording fees (2026):</div>
          <div>• $30 for the first page</div>
          <div>• $3 for each additional page</div>
          <div class="mt-2 text-[#6B6560]">A typical affidavit runs 2–4 pages: budget $30–$42.</div>
          <div class="mt-2 text-[#6B6560]">Mail accepted — include a self-addressed stamped envelope for return of stamped document.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="mt-4 p-3 bg-[#F7F4EF] rounded-lg border border-[#D4CCC0] text-sm text-[#6B6560]">
        <strong>Note:</strong> This is a Recording, not a court filing — bring your completed, notarized affidavit. You do not file a petition with the Superior Court.
      </div>
    </div>

    <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl text-[#2D5016] mb-4">Step-by-Step: Maricopa County Real Property Process</h2>
    <div class="space-y-4 mb-8">
      <div class="flex gap-4 p-4 bg-[#EEE9E1] rounded-lg border border-[#D4CCC0]">
        <div class="w-8 h-8 bg-[#2D5016] text-[#F7F4EF] rounded-full flex items-center justify-center font-serif text-sm shrink-0 mt-0.5">1</div>
        <div>
          <strong class="text-[#2D5016]">Confirm the 30-day waiting period</strong>
          <p class="text-[#2C2C2A] text-sm mt-1">Check the date of death on the death certificate. You cannot record the affidavit until at least 30 days after that date.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="flex gap-4 p-4 bg-[#EEE9E1] rounded-lg border border-[#D4CCC0]">
        <div class="w-8 h-8 bg-[#2D5016] text-[#F7F4EF] rounded-full flex items-center justify-center font-serif text-sm shrink-0 mt-0.5">2</div>
        <div>
          <strong class="text-[#2D5016]">Verify the property value</strong>
          <p class="text-[#2C2C2A] text-sm mt-1">Determine the current fair market value of the real property. If it exceeds $300,000, formal probate or another transfer mechanism is needed.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="flex gap-4 p-4 bg-[#EEE9E1] rounded-lg border border-[#D4CCC0]">
        <div class="w-8 h-8 bg-[#2D5016] text-[#F7F4EF] rounded-full flex items-center justify-center font-serif text-sm shrink-0 mt-0.5">3</div>
        <div>
          <strong class="text-[#2D5016]">Use the correct form</strong>
          <p class="text-[#2C2C2A] text-sm mt-1">Maricopa County recommends forms consistent with the Maricopa Superior Court's probate division. Available through the Maricopa County Superior Court Self-Service Center, or generated by smallestateform.com (which auto-selects Maricopa County forms).</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="flex gap-4 p-4 bg-[#EEE9E1] rounded-lg border border-[#D4CCC0]">
        <div class="w-8 h-8 bg-[#2D5016] text-[#F7F4EF] rounded-full flex items-center justify-center font-serif text-sm shrink-0 mt-0.5">4</div>
        <div>
          <strong class="text-[#2D5016]">Include required elements</strong>
          <div class="text-[#2C2C2A] text-sm mt-1 space-y-1">
            <div>✓ Deceased's full name and date of death</div>
            <div>✓ Statement that 30 days have elapsed</div>
            <div>✓ Statement that no probate proceeding is pending</div>
            <div>✓ Legal property description (not just the address)</div>
            <div>✓ Fair market value statement</div>
            <div>✓ Affiant's relationship to the deceased</div>
            <div>✓ Names of all known heirs</div>
            <div>✓ Notarized signature</div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="flex gap-4 p-4 bg-[#EEE9E1] rounded-lg border border-[#D4CCC0]">
        <div class="w-8 h-8 bg-[#2D5016] text-[#F7F4EF] rounded-full flex items-center justify-center font-serif text-sm shrink-0 mt-0.5">5</div>
        <div>
          <strong class="text-[#2D5016]">Notarize</strong>
          <p class="text-[#2C2C2A] text-sm mt-1">Sign the affidavit in front of a notary public. The Maricopa County Recorder will not accept an unnotarized affidavit.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="flex gap-4 p-4 bg-[#EEE9E1] rounded-lg border border-[#D4CCC0]">
        <div class="w-8 h-8 bg-[#2D5016] text-[#F7F4EF] rounded-full flex items-center justify-center font-serif text-sm shrink-0 mt-0.5">6</div>
        <div>
          <strong class="text-[#2D5016]">Record at the Maricopa County Recorder</strong>
          <p class="text-[#2C2C2A] text-sm mt-1">Bring or mail the original notarized affidavit to 111 S 3rd Ave, Phoenix, AZ 85003. Pay the $30 first-page fee plus $3 per additional page.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="flex gap-4 p-4 bg-[#EEE9E1] rounded-lg border border-[#D4CCC0]">
        <div class="w-8 h-8 bg-[#2D5016] text-[#F7F4EF] rounded-full flex items-center justify-center font-serif text-sm shrink-0 mt-0.5">7</div>
        <div>
          <strong class="text-[#2D5016]">Obtain certified copies</strong>
          <p class="text-[#2C2C2A] text-sm mt-1">Request certified copies after recording. Banks and title companies require certified copies — not photocopies — when accepting the recorded affidavit as proof of transfer.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl text-[#2D5016] mb-4">What to Bring to the Maricopa County Recorder</h2>
    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-6 mb-8">
      <div class="border border-[#D4CCC0] rounded-xl p-5 bg-[#F7F4EF]">
        <h3 class="font-medium text-[#2D5016] mb-3">Filing in person</h3>
        <ul class="text-sm text-[#2C2C2A] space-y-2">
          <li>• Original notarized Small Estate Affidavit</li>
          <li>• Copy of the death certificate (useful to have)</li>
          <li>• Payment for recording fees (check, money order, or exact cash; confirm card acceptance)</li>
          <li>• Self-addressed stamped envelope if you want a stamped copy returned</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="border border-[#D4CCC0] rounded-xl p-5 bg-[#F7F4EF]">
        <h3 class="font-medium text-[#2D5016] mb-3">Filing by mail</h3>
        <ul class="text-sm text-[#2C2C2A] space-y-2">
          <li>• Original notarized affidavit</li>
          <li>• Check payable to "Maricopa County Recorder" for the correct fee</li>
          <li>• Self-addressed stamped envelope</li>
          <li>• Note requesting a conformed copy be mailed back</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>

  </div>

  <!-- FAQ -->
  <section class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6 pb-16">
    <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl text-[#2D5016] mb-8">Frequently asked questions: Maricopa County</h2>
    <div class="space-y-4">

      <details class="bg-[#EEE9E1] rounded-xl border border-[#D4CCC0]">
        <summary class="p-6 font-medium text-[#2D5016] cursor-pointer list-none flex items-center justify-between">
          Do I file the Maricopa County Small Estate Affidavit at the Superior Court or the Recorder?
          <span class="text-[#8B6914] text-xl">+</span>
        </summary>
        <div class="px-6 pb-6 text-[#2C2C2A] leading-relaxed text-sm">
          For real property, you record with the <strong>Maricopa County Recorder</strong> (111 S 3rd Ave, Phoenix). You do not file a petition with the Superior Court — the affidavit process is designed to bypass the court entirely. The Recorder records the transfer; the Court is not involved unless there is a dispute.
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="bg-[#EEE9E1] rounded-xl border border-[#D4CCC0]">
        <summary class="p-6 font-medium text-[#2D5016] cursor-pointer list-none flex items-center justify-between">
          What if the estate has both personal property and real property in Maricopa County?
          <span class="text-[#8B6914] text-xl">+</span>
        </summary>
        <div class="px-6 pb-6 text-[#2C2C2A] leading-relaxed text-sm">
          Each is handled separately. Personal property (bank accounts, vehicles) — present the affidavit to the institution. Real property — record with the Maricopa County Recorder. Each must independently fall within its respective threshold ($200,000 personal, $300,000 real).
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="bg-[#EEE9E1] rounded-xl border border-[#D4CCC0]">
        <summary class="p-6 font-medium text-[#2D5016] cursor-pointer list-none flex items-center justify-between">
          Can I file by mail with the Maricopa County Recorder?
          <span class="text-[#8B6914] text-xl">+</span>
        </summary>
        <div class="px-6 pb-6 text-[#2C2C2A] leading-relaxed text-sm">
          Yes. Mail the original notarized affidavit with payment and a self-addressed stamped envelope to 111 S 3rd Ave, Phoenix, AZ 85003. Turnaround is typically 1–3 business days after receipt.
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="bg-[#EEE9E1] rounded-xl border border-[#D4CCC0]">
        <summary class="p-6 font-medium text-[#2D5016] cursor-pointer list-none flex items-center justify-between">
          What are the current Maricopa County small estate thresholds?
          <span class="text-[#8B6914] text-xl">+</span>
        </summary>
        <div class="px-6 pb-6 text-[#2C2C2A] leading-relaxed text-sm">
          As of March 2026 under HB2116: $200,000 for personal property and $300,000 for real property. A 30-day waiting period applies. If you've seen lower numbers elsewhere, the source is outdated.
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="bg-[#EEE9E1] rounded-xl border border-[#D4CCC0]">
        <summary class="p-6 font-medium text-[#2D5016] cursor-pointer list-none flex items-center justify-between">
          What if someone disputes my claim to the property?
          <span class="text-[#8B6914] text-xl">+</span>
        </summary>
        <div class="px-6 pb-6 text-[#2C2C2A] leading-relaxed text-sm">
          A Small Estate Affidavit does not extinguish other heirs' legal claims. If there is a legitimate dispute among heirs, the affidavit process should be approached cautiously — a disputed estate is generally better handled through formal probate where a court can adjudicate the competing claims.
        </div>
      </details>

    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Internal links -->
  <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6 pb-12 text-sm text-[#6B6560]">
    Related: <a href="/small-estate-affidavit/arizona/" class="text-[#8B6914] hover:underline">Arizona Small Estate Affidavit (statewide)</a> | <a href="/small-estate-affidavit/" class="text-[#8B6914] hover:underline">Small Estate Affidavit Hub</a> | <a href="/" class="text-[#8B6914] hover:underline">Homepage</a>
  </div>

</StatePage>
